      "headline": "Why Your Past Does Not Control Your Steering Wheel",
      "keyInsight": "We love treating our past like a fixed GPS route that forces where we go today. If something went wrong years ago, we claim our present engine is permanently damaged. But here is the jaw-dropping twist from Alfred Adler: you are not driven by past causes. You are driven by present goals! Think about a person who refuses to leave the house because of past anxiety. Common sense says the fear forces them inside. Adler points out the reverse gear: they invent the fear right now so they have an ironclad excuse to stay safe in bed! We do not suffer from the shock of our experiences. Instead, we hunt through our memories, pick out handy old parts, and weld together an alibi to dodge a scary challenge today. Your history is not a heavy brick wall trapping you. It is merely a scrapbook you keep flipping through to avoid stepping outside into the rain.",
      "content": "Imagine you spill coffee on your white shirt right before a big job interview. You can either grab a wet towel and scrub it out, or you can storm into the lobby yelling that the coffee cup ruined your entire career. Most of us pick the second option with our lives. We treat ancient mistakes and bad days like permanent stains that control our future. Psychologists used to teach that life is like a chain of falling dominoes. A bad nudge in childhood knocks over your confidence today, leaving you completely powerless.\n\nNow look at your car dashboard instead. When you step on the gas pedal, the car moves forward because of the pressure you apply right now, not because of where you drove last summer. Adlerian psychology looks under the hood of human behavior and reveals a shocking loop: we do not act because of past causes, we act to fulfill hidden present goals. If you convince yourself you are hopelessly shy because of an awkward moment in college, that shyness serves a very cozy purpose today. It protects your pride! As long as you believe you are defective, you never have to ask anyone out or risk getting rejected at a mixer.\n\nEven sudden bursts of temper work this way. Imagine you are shouting at a waiter for dropping soup, and suddenly your phone rings with a call from your boss. Within half a second, your voice switches to sweet, polite, and calm. The moment you hang up, you switch right back to roaring at the waiter! You did not lose control of your anger like a broken steam valve. You manufactured the anger on the spot as a handy tool to shout down the waiter and get your way. You hold the controls in every single room you enter.",
