      "headline": "Why Every Headache Comes Down to Other People",
      "keyInsight": "If you were the sole human being living on planet Earth, lonely worries would instantly evaporate. You would never worry about earning less money, having bad hair, or walking too slowly. Why? Because there would be nobody around to outshine you! All personal misery springs from the quiet measuring tape we stretch between ourselves and our neighbors. We look at someone else's garden, notice their tomatoes are bigger, and convince ourselves our soil is worthless. Worse yet, we turn everyday life into a marathon race where anyone passing us feels like an enemy. When you view society as an endless scoreboard, every friend's promotion feels like your personal demotion. But reality is not a vertical ladder where someone stands on your fingers. It is a wide, flat plain where everyone simply walks at their own natural pace. You are not competing against anyone; you are only moving forward relative to yourself.",
      "content": "Picture sitting alone inside a quiet cabin deep in the woods with enough food for a lifetime. In that cabin, you have zero worries about wearing trendy shoes, losing an argument, or sounding silly. The moment another hiker knocks on the cabin door, a dozen new anxieties flash to life! Will they judge your messy kitchen? Are they stronger, smarter, or wealthier than you? Every single struggle we face is secretly tied to our connections with other humans.\n\nWe usually try to fix this discomfort using two broken tools: feeling inferior or acting superior. When we feel shorter or less successful than our peers, healthy ambition nudges us to practice and grow. But many people take a cheap shortcut. Instead of doing the hard work to improve, they brag about their fancy watch, drop names of famous acquaintances, or constantly complain about their rotten luck. Complaining is actually a sneaky bid for power! By showing off your wounds, you make everyone else walk on eggshells around you.\n\nThis turns the entire neighborhood into a stressful boxing ring. If your coworker lands a bonus, your stomach knots up because you think you just lost a round. But other people are not rivals trying to trip you up on the sidewalk. They are simply fellow travelers sharing the same road. When you drop the measuring tape and step off the imaginary competition podium, you can celebrate their wins as easily as your own morning coffee.",
