      "chapterNumber": 4,
      "headline": "You Are Not the Main Character on Earth",
      "keyInsight": "We often think people watch our every step. We act like movie directors staring through a camera lens. But here is the secret joke of human nature: nobody is watching your show. When you constantly worry about what others think of you, you are not actually thinking about them. You are totally obsessed with yourself. Adlerian psychology points out a bizarre paradox: self-conscious people are often the most self-centered people alive. You worry if your hair looks bad. You worry if your presentation sounded goofy. That means you think the universe revolves around your forehead. True peace begins the moment you resign as the center of the galaxy. You are simply one team member sitting at the giant dining table of life. Stop demanding applause from the room. Start wondering what dish you can bring to the potluck instead.",
      "content": "Imagine you walk into a busy train station wearing mismatched shoes. Your heart beats fast. You assume every commuter is secretly judging your fashion choices. But take a look around the platform. One commuter is running late for work. Another is chewing a cold bagel. A third is arguing with their phone. Nobody noticed your shoes. When we enter a room, we tend to picture ourselves standing on a brightly lit concert stage. In reality, we are just sitting in the audience alongside everyone else.\n\nAdler calls this the shift from self-obsession to community feeling. Think of a healthy garden. A single tomato plant cannot command the entire ecosystem. It does not demand that the bees only visit its blossoms. It simply digs its roots into the shared soil. It soaks up sunlight and quietly feeds the patch. When you treat relationships like a stage performance, you turn everyone into your judge. That creates endless anxiety.\n\nTo break this invisible loop, switch from a vertical ladder to a horizontal plain. On a ladder, you constantly look up in envy or look down in judgment. On a horizontal plain, everyone walks side by side. Nobody is above you, and nobody is beneath you. When a colleague does a wonderful job, do not offer patronizing praise like a boss patting a worker. Treat them as an equal partner. Simply say \"thank you\" and celebrate their contribution.\n\nBelonging is not something you demand from the world. It is something you build through active participation. When you stop obsessing over your reflection in every window, the whole landscape suddenly opens up before your eyes.",
